0) = 0\n- F(1) = 1\n- F(n) = F(n-1) + F(n-2) for n > 1\n\nHere is the iterative implementation:\n\n```python\ndef fibonacci(n):\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    \n    prev = 0  # F(0)\n    curr = 1  # F(1)\n    \n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        next_val = prev + curr\n        prev = curr\n        curr = next_val\n    \n    return curr\n```\n\n**Time Complexity Analysis:**\n\n1. **Base cases (lines 2–4):** These are constant-time operations. Each check and return takes O(1) time.\n2. **Initialization (lines 6–7):** Assigning values to `prev` and `curr` is also O(1).\n3. **Loop (line 9):** The loop runs from `i = 2` to `n`, executing exactly `n - 1` times. Inside the loop:"}
